  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When sinusoidal voltage is applied across&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;circuit, current is also sinusoidal but it lags by some phase.</p><p>Current will be in the form of<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>sin</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow></mfenced></math><br />Graph will be sinusoidal.</p><p>Here&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;the maximum current and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi></math>&nbsp;is phase difference between&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>I</mi></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
